1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,370
Listen. Practice. Speak. Practice makes you perfect.

2
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:17,390
Hello! We're really happy to have you with us today.

3
00:00:17,390 --> 00:00:23,390
Hi everyone! Thanks for joining us. If you're here to improve your English, you're in the right place. I'm Sarah.

4
00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:30,390
And I'm Daniel. On this channel, we help you speak clear, natural English with confidence in real life situations.

5
00:00:30,390 --> 00:00:35,390
If you enjoy this video, please give us a thumbs up and don't forget to subscribe.

6
00:00:35,390 --> 00:00:39,390
Yes, your support helps us so much. Thank you for being here.

7
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:41,890
So Sarah, what are we talking about today?

8
00:00:41,890 --> 00:00:44,390
Today we have a very practical topic.

9
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:48,890
We're going to learn how to use negotiation phrases in business meetings.

10
00:00:48,890 --> 00:00:51,390
Oh, this is such an important skill.

11
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:54,390
Negotiation comes up in many situations,

12
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:59,390
whether you're discussing a contract, agreeing on deadlines, or even talking about prices.

13
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:03,390
Exactly. And we're going to make it easy and practical.

14
00:01:03,390 --> 00:01:09,470
We'll teach you useful phrases, common mistakes to avoid, and some practice dialogues.

15
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:10,470
Sounds perfect.

16
00:01:10,470 --> 00:01:13,270
So, Sarah, before we start, how are you feeling today?

17
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:14,430
I'm feeling great!

18
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:18,430
I had a productive morning preparing examples for today's lesson.

19
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:19,430
How about you?

20
00:01:19,430 --> 00:01:20,430
I'm excited too.

21
00:01:20,430 --> 00:01:24,830
I think our viewers will find this really useful, especially for meetings at work or

22
00:01:24,830 --> 00:01:25,990
with clients.

23
00:01:25,990 --> 00:01:26,990
I agree.

24
00:01:26,990 --> 00:01:29,110
Okay, everyone, let's get started.

25
00:01:29,110 --> 00:01:32,310
All right, Daniel, let's start with the basics.

26
00:01:32,310 --> 00:01:35,390
How do we start a negotiation in a business meeting?

27
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:36,710
Good question.

28
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:40,350
A common way to start is by setting a positive tone.

29
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:44,910
You can say something like, thank you for meeting with me today, or I'm glad we could

30
00:01:44,910 --> 00:01:46,830
find time to discuss this.

31
00:01:46,830 --> 00:01:48,790
That sounds polite and professional.

32
00:01:48,790 --> 00:01:50,070
I like that.

33
00:01:50,070 --> 00:01:51,470
Any other ways to start?

34
00:01:51,470 --> 00:01:57,550
Yes, you can also set the context by saying, I'd like to talk about our current contract,

35
00:01:57,550 --> 00:02:00,670
or I'd like to discuss the project budget.

36
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:02,670
It's direct, but polite.

37
00:02:02,670 --> 00:02:06,670
So we start with a greeting, then a statement about the topic.

38
00:02:06,670 --> 00:02:07,670
Got it.

39
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:08,670
Exactly.

40
00:02:08,670 --> 00:02:12,830
Now, once the meeting starts, you might need to express your position.

41
00:02:12,830 --> 00:02:18,010
For example, if you're negotiating a price, you can say, we'd like to propose a rate

42
00:02:18,010 --> 00:02:20,710
of $5,000 for this project.

43
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:23,110
Can you give another example for deadlines?

44
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:24,110
Sure.

45
00:02:24,110 --> 00:02:29,150
You could say, we'd like to complete the project within six weeks, if possible.

46
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:31,950
Notice how it's polite but still shows your goal.

47
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:33,350
Ah, I see.

48
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:35,630
It's about being clear but respectful.

49
00:02:35,630 --> 00:02:36,630
Right.

50
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:39,710
And you can also ask the other party for their opinion.

51
00:02:39,710 --> 00:02:42,950
For example, does this timeline work for you?

52
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:45,230
Or what are your thoughts on the budget?

53
00:02:45,230 --> 00:02:48,270
These phrases make the conversation more collaborative.

54
00:02:48,270 --> 00:02:49,670
Exactly.

55
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:52,630
Instead of sounding bossy, you invite discussion.

56
00:02:52,630 --> 00:02:57,630
Okay, Sarah, what about when the other person makes a suggestion or counter offer?

57
00:02:57,630 --> 00:02:58,630
Good question.

58
00:02:58,630 --> 00:03:01,310
First, acknowledge what they said.

59
00:03:01,310 --> 00:03:05,810
You can say, I understand your point, or that's a good suggestion.

60
00:03:05,810 --> 00:03:07,430
Even if you don't agree yet?

61
00:03:07,430 --> 00:03:08,430
Yes.

62
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:10,310
Acknowledgement shows respect.

63
00:03:10,310 --> 00:03:12,710
After that, you can state your position.

64
00:03:12,710 --> 00:03:18,230
For example, I see your offer, but could we consider $5,500 instead?

65
00:03:18,230 --> 00:03:19,430
I like that.

66
00:03:19,430 --> 00:03:23,270
So you agree in part, then suggest an alternative.

67
00:03:23,270 --> 00:03:24,550
Exactly.

68
00:03:24,550 --> 00:03:28,870
Another useful phrase is, would you be open to adjusting this slightly?

69
00:03:28,870 --> 00:03:33,510
And for timelines, you might say, I understand the deadline you suggested.

70
00:03:33,510 --> 00:03:36,630
Could we extend it by one week to ensure quality?

71
00:03:36,630 --> 00:03:39,310
Yes, that's polite, but clear.

72
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:41,950
Another way is to use softening words.

73
00:03:41,950 --> 00:03:47,870
Words like maybe, perhaps, or possibly can help make requests less direct.

74
00:03:47,870 --> 00:03:51,270
For example, perhaps we could discuss a higher budget.

75
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:52,270
I see.

76
00:03:52,270 --> 00:03:53,710
That sounds more diplomatic.

77
00:03:53,710 --> 00:03:58,990
Daniel, I often hear people using I THINK in negotiations. Is that natural?

78
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:05,150
Yes, but it depends on the tone. For example, I think we might consider extending the deadline

79
00:04:05,150 --> 00:04:09,310
is polite, but if you overuse it, you may sound unsure.

80
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:13,390
Ah, balance is key. Not too soft, not too aggressive.

81
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:20,350
Exactly. Another useful phrase is, we're willing to be flexible on X if you can adjust Y.

82
00:04:20,350 --> 00:04:23,150
Oh, that's great. Can you give a full example?

83
00:04:23,150 --> 00:04:28,910
Sure. We're willing to be flexible on the delivery date if you can increase the budget slightly.

84
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:31,630
I like that. It shows compromise.

85
00:04:31,630 --> 00:04:38,190
Yes, and compromise is essential in negotiations. Speaking of which, another helpful phrase is,

86
00:04:38,190 --> 00:04:40,990
let's find a solution that works for both of us.

87
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:46,110
That's very collaborative. It invites cooperation rather than confrontation.

88
00:04:46,110 --> 00:04:50,270
Exactly. And if you need more time to think, you can say,

89
00:04:50,270 --> 00:04:53,850
I'd like to review this proposal and get back to you.

90
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:55,050
Very useful.

91
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:58,490
Sometimes negotiations get heated and it's good to pause.

92
00:04:58,490 --> 00:05:01,710
Okay, Sarah, what about ending the negotiation?

93
00:05:01,710 --> 00:05:04,210
How do you close the conversation politely?

94
00:05:04,210 --> 00:05:10,650
You can summarize the agreement by saying, so we've agreed on or to confirm will proceed

95
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:11,650
with...

96
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:13,490
Can you give a practical example?

97
00:05:13,490 --> 00:05:14,490
Sure.

98
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:20,730
To confirm, we'll deliver the project in six weeks and the agreed rate is $5,500.

99
00:05:20,730 --> 00:05:22,230
That's very clear.

100
00:05:22,230 --> 00:05:24,430
And if there's no agreement yet?

101
00:05:24,430 --> 00:05:30,150
Then you can say, let's continue this discussion later, or I think we need more time to review

102
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:31,370
the options.

103
00:05:31,370 --> 00:05:35,210
It keeps the conversation professional without ending abruptly.

104
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:36,210
Exactly.

105
00:05:36,210 --> 00:05:38,690
And always thank the person at the end.

106
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:41,930
Thank you for your time, or I appreciate your input.

107
00:05:41,930 --> 00:05:46,890
Sara, can we practice a role play for a negotiation about project pricing?

108
00:05:46,890 --> 00:05:47,890
Great idea!

109
00:05:47,890 --> 00:05:51,090
Let's imagine I'm a client and you're the project manager.

110
00:05:51,090 --> 00:05:52,090
Perfect.

111
00:05:52,090 --> 00:05:53,090
I'll start.

112
00:05:53,090 --> 00:05:54,090
Hi, D'Angelo.

113
00:05:54,090 --> 00:05:55,170
Thank you for meeting me today.

114
00:05:55,170 --> 00:05:57,410
I'm excited to discuss the new project.

115
00:05:57,410 --> 00:05:58,410
Hi, Sara.

116
00:05:58,410 --> 00:05:59,410
Thanks for having me.

117
00:05:59,410 --> 00:06:01,410
I'm glad we could find time to talk.

118
00:06:01,410 --> 00:06:06,130
So regarding the budget, we'd like to propose $5,000 for this project.

119
00:06:06,130 --> 00:06:07,130
I see.

120
00:06:07,130 --> 00:06:11,810
I was hoping the budget could be closer to $6,000 given the scope of work.

121
00:06:11,810 --> 00:06:14,170
I understand. That's a fair point.

122
00:06:14,170 --> 00:06:17,450
Would you be open to $5,500 as a compromise?

123
00:06:17,450 --> 00:06:20,770
Hmm, $5,500 is reasonable.

124
00:06:20,770 --> 00:06:23,530
However, could we possibly adjust the timeline

125
00:06:23,530 --> 00:06:25,970
to seven weeks to ensure quality?

126
00:06:25,970 --> 00:06:28,130
Yes, we can extend the timeline.

127
00:06:28,130 --> 00:06:29,090
Let's confirm.

128
00:06:29,090 --> 00:06:33,490
The budget will be $5,500, and the deadline is seven weeks.

129
00:06:33,490 --> 00:06:34,610
That works for me.

130
00:06:34,610 --> 00:06:35,610
Thank you, Sarah.

131
00:06:35,610 --> 00:06:36,530
Thank you, Daniel.

132
00:06:36,530 --> 00:06:38,370
I appreciate your flexibility.

133
00:06:38,370 --> 00:06:41,090
See how simple phrases made this negotiation

134
00:06:41,090 --> 00:06:42,650
smooth and professional?

135
00:06:42,650 --> 00:06:47,690
Yes. Acknowledgement, compromise, and polite suggestions really help.

136
00:06:47,690 --> 00:06:52,790
Sarah, can we do another scenario about negotiating working hours or deadlines?

137
00:06:52,790 --> 00:06:58,690
Sure. This time, let's imagine a team leader and an employee negotiating a deadline.

138
00:06:58,690 --> 00:07:00,990
Okay. I'll be the team leader.

139
00:07:00,990 --> 00:07:04,590
Hi, Alex. I'd like to discuss the deadline for the report.

140
00:07:04,590 --> 00:07:08,990
Hi, Sarah. Sure. I noticed the deadline is set for next Monday.

141
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:12,630
I think we might need an extra two days to ensure accuracy.

142
00:07:12,630 --> 00:07:14,350
I see your point.

143
00:07:14,350 --> 00:07:15,830
Accuracy is important.

144
00:07:15,830 --> 00:07:20,830
Could we possibly extend it to Wednesday instead of Friday to meet your concerns?

145
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:21,830
Wednesday works.

146
00:07:21,830 --> 00:07:22,830
Thank you.

147
00:07:22,830 --> 00:07:23,830
I appreciate the flexibility.

148
00:07:23,830 --> 00:07:24,830
Great.

149
00:07:24,830 --> 00:07:25,830
Let's agree.

150
00:07:25,830 --> 00:07:30,390
The report will be due Wednesday, and you'll send me a draft by Monday for review.

151
00:07:30,390 --> 00:07:31,390
Perfect.

152
00:07:31,390 --> 00:07:33,550
That gives us both time to check details.

153
00:07:33,550 --> 00:07:34,550
Thank you.

154
00:07:34,550 --> 00:07:35,550
Excellent.

155
00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:37,110
Notice how we used polite phrases.

156
00:07:37,110 --> 00:07:41,110
I think we might need. And could we possibly extend?

157
00:07:41,110 --> 00:07:44,310
Yes, they soften the request while being professional.

158
00:07:44,310 --> 00:07:48,310
Hi everyone, thanks for joining us. If you're here to improve your business

159
00:07:48,310 --> 00:07:51,110
English, you're definitely in the right place.

160
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:54,950
I'm Alex. And I'm Sarah. On this channel, we help you speak

161
00:07:54,950 --> 00:07:59,750
clear, natural English with confidence in real life business situations.

162
00:07:59,750 --> 00:08:02,950
If you enjoy this lesson, don't forget to like and subscribe.

163
00:08:02,950 --> 00:08:06,950
Your support really helps us keep creating more content like this.

164
00:08:06,950 --> 00:08:07,950
Absolutely.

165
00:08:07,950 --> 00:08:10,050
Thank you so much for being here.

166
00:08:10,050 --> 00:08:12,750
So Alex, what are we talking about today?

167
00:08:12,750 --> 00:08:15,750
Today's topic is extremely practical and powerful.

168
00:08:15,750 --> 00:08:19,830
We're going to learn how to use negotiation phrases in business meetings.

169
00:08:19,830 --> 00:08:21,870
This is such an important skill.

170
00:08:21,870 --> 00:08:27,790
Whether you're discussing prices, deadlines, contracts, or responsibilities, negotiation

171
00:08:27,790 --> 00:08:30,070
is everywhere in business.

172
00:08:30,070 --> 00:08:31,070
Exactly.

173
00:08:31,070 --> 00:08:33,950
And the good news is, you don't need complicated vocabulary.

174
00:08:33,950 --> 00:08:36,670
You just need the right phrases and the right tone.

175
00:08:36,670 --> 00:08:42,130
Yes, and today we're going to give you practical, real-world examples so you can actually use

176
00:08:42,130 --> 00:08:44,330
these phrases in your next meeting.

177
00:08:44,330 --> 00:08:48,550
We'll also practice together and show you how real conversations happen.

178
00:08:48,550 --> 00:08:49,550
Sounds great!

179
00:08:49,550 --> 00:08:52,550
So before we start, Alex, how are you feeling today?

180
00:08:52,550 --> 00:08:53,750
I'm feeling great.

181
00:08:53,750 --> 00:08:55,690
Ready to negotiate everything today.

182
00:08:55,690 --> 00:08:56,690
How about you?

183
00:08:56,690 --> 00:08:57,690
I'm good.

184
00:08:57,690 --> 00:09:01,030
Let's see if you can negotiate your way through this lesson.

185
00:09:01,030 --> 00:09:02,030
Challenge accepted.

186
00:09:02,030 --> 00:09:03,990
All right, everyone, let's get started.

187
00:09:03,990 --> 00:09:06,450
So Sarah, let's begin with the basics.

188
00:09:06,450 --> 00:09:08,850
What is negotiation in business?

189
00:09:08,850 --> 00:09:10,730
Good question.

190
00:09:10,730 --> 00:09:15,210
Negotiation is when two or more people discuss something to reach an agreement.

191
00:09:15,210 --> 00:09:17,930
So it's not about winning or losing, right?

192
00:09:17,930 --> 00:09:18,930
Exactly.

193
00:09:18,930 --> 00:09:22,210
It's about finding a solution that works for everyone.

194
00:09:22,210 --> 00:09:23,210
That's important.

195
00:09:23,210 --> 00:09:27,610
Many people think negotiation means arguing, but it's actually about collaboration.

196
00:09:27,610 --> 00:09:31,330
Right, and the language you use can make a big difference.

197
00:09:31,330 --> 00:09:34,410
Okay, so let's start with a simple situation.

198
00:09:34,410 --> 00:09:38,190
When you're in a meeting and you want to make a suggestion, what can you say?

199
00:09:38,190 --> 00:09:44,070
You can start with polite phrases like, I'd like to suggest, or can I propose something?

200
00:09:44,070 --> 00:09:45,350
That sounds professional.

201
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:46,350
Yes.

202
00:09:46,350 --> 00:09:50,670
For example, I'd like to suggest we extend the deadline by one week.

203
00:09:50,670 --> 00:09:53,710
Or can I propose a different approach to this project?

204
00:09:53,710 --> 00:09:54,710
Exactly.

205
00:09:54,710 --> 00:09:58,030
These phrases show confidence, but also respect.

206
00:09:58,030 --> 00:10:00,390
What if you want to ask for someone's opinion?

207
00:10:00,390 --> 00:10:05,710
You can say, what do you think about this, or how do you feel about this proposal?

208
00:10:05,710 --> 00:10:09,190
That's useful because negotiation is a two-way conversation.

209
00:10:09,190 --> 00:10:12,650
Yes, you're not just talking, you're listening too.

210
00:10:12,650 --> 00:10:14,670
Now let's talk about agreeing.

211
00:10:14,670 --> 00:10:16,310
That's the easy part, right?

212
00:10:16,310 --> 00:10:17,670
Usually yes.

213
00:10:17,670 --> 00:10:21,530
You can say, I agree, or that sounds good to me.

214
00:10:21,530 --> 00:10:25,390
Or something more formal like, I think that's a reasonable solution.

215
00:10:25,390 --> 00:10:26,390
Exactly.

216
00:10:26,390 --> 00:10:30,270
You can also say, I'm happy with that, or that works for me.

217
00:10:30,270 --> 00:10:31,750
Simple and clear.

218
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:33,710
Now comes the tricky part.

219
00:10:33,710 --> 00:10:34,710
Disagreying.

220
00:10:34,710 --> 00:10:35,910
Ah, yes.

221
00:10:35,910 --> 00:10:38,550
This is where many learners feel uncomfortable.

222
00:10:38,550 --> 00:10:41,690
But the key is to be polite and professional.

223
00:10:41,690 --> 00:10:45,050
So instead of saying, that's wrong, what should we say?

224
00:10:45,050 --> 00:10:48,250
You can say, I see your point, but...

225
00:10:48,250 --> 00:10:49,250
That's a great phrase.

226
00:10:49,250 --> 00:10:51,090
It softens the disagreement.

227
00:10:51,090 --> 00:10:55,570
For example, I see your point, but I think we need more time.

228
00:10:55,570 --> 00:10:59,550
Or I understand what you're saying, however, I have a different perspective.

229
00:10:59,550 --> 00:11:03,550
Exactly. You're showing respect while still expressing your opinion.

230
00:11:03,550 --> 00:11:06,550
What if you want to negotiate price or budget?

231
00:11:06,550 --> 00:11:09,550
That's very common in business meetings. You can say,

232
00:11:09,550 --> 00:11:12,550
is there any flexibility in the budget?

233
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:14,550
Nice. That sounds polite and professional.

234
00:11:14,550 --> 00:11:18,550
Or would it be possible to adjust the price?

235
00:11:18,550 --> 00:11:20,550
That's better than saying it's too expensive.

236
00:11:20,550 --> 00:11:23,550
Exactly. Always soften your language.

237
00:11:23,550 --> 00:11:28,550
Let's say someone gives you an offer. How do you respond if you want a better deal?

238
00:11:28,550 --> 00:11:33,110
You can say, that's a good starting point, but could we discuss the terms further?

239
00:11:33,110 --> 00:11:34,110
I like that.

240
00:11:34,110 --> 00:11:36,030
It keeps the conversation open.

241
00:11:36,030 --> 00:11:39,350
Or I was hoping for something closer to…

242
00:11:39,350 --> 00:11:43,590
For example, I was hoping for something closer to 50,000 pounds.

243
00:11:43,590 --> 00:11:45,590
Yes, very natural.

244
00:11:45,590 --> 00:11:47,670
What about making a counter offer?

245
00:11:47,670 --> 00:11:50,230
You can say, would you consider…

246
00:11:50,230 --> 00:11:51,350
Example?

247
00:11:51,350 --> 00:11:55,110
Would you consider extending the contract to 12 months?

248
00:11:55,110 --> 00:11:58,990
Or would you consider a lower price if we increase the order?

249
00:11:58,990 --> 00:11:59,990
Perfect.

250
00:11:59,990 --> 00:12:02,670
That's real negotiation language.

251
00:12:02,670 --> 00:12:04,630
Now let's talk about compromise.

252
00:12:04,630 --> 00:12:05,790
Yes.

253
00:12:05,790 --> 00:12:08,270
This is the heart of negotiation.

254
00:12:08,270 --> 00:12:10,430
What's a good phrase for compromise?

255
00:12:10,430 --> 00:12:13,870
You can say, we're willing to compromise on…

256
00:12:13,870 --> 00:12:17,470
For example, we're willing to compromise on the delivery date.

257
00:12:17,470 --> 00:12:18,470
Exactly.

258
00:12:18,470 --> 00:12:21,670
Or if you can do this, we can do that.

259
00:12:21,670 --> 00:12:23,190
That's a powerful structure.

260
00:12:23,190 --> 00:12:24,190
Yes.

261
00:12:24,190 --> 00:12:28,190
For example, if you can reduce the price, we can increase the order.

262
00:12:28,190 --> 00:12:30,190
That's negotiation in action.

263
00:12:30,190 --> 00:12:32,190
Let's do a quick role play.

264
00:12:32,190 --> 00:12:35,190
Great idea. I'll be the client. You be the supplier.

265
00:12:35,190 --> 00:12:36,190
Perfect.

266
00:12:36,190 --> 00:12:40,190
Thank you for your proposal. The price is a bit higher than we expected.

267
00:12:40,190 --> 00:12:43,190
I understand. Could you tell me your budget range?

268
00:12:43,190 --> 00:12:47,190
We were hoping for something closer to 10,000 pounds.

269
00:12:47,190 --> 00:12:52,190
I see your point. If you're able to commit to a longer contract, we could adjust the price.

270
00:12:52,190 --> 00:12:56,190
That sounds reasonable. What kind of discount can you offer?

271
00:12:56,190 --> 00:13:00,190
We could reduce it by 10% if the contract is for 12 months.

272
00:13:00,190 --> 00:13:02,190
That's interesting. Let me think about it.

273
00:13:02,190 --> 00:13:04,190
Of course. Take your time.

274
00:13:04,190 --> 00:13:06,190
That was very realistic.

275
00:13:06,190 --> 00:13:10,190
Yes, and notice how polite and calm the conversation was.

276
00:13:10,190 --> 00:13:13,190
No pressure, no conflict, just discussion.

277
00:13:13,190 --> 00:13:17,190
Let's continue with more phrases. What if you need time to think?

278
00:13:17,190 --> 00:13:19,190
That happens a lot.

279
00:13:19,190 --> 00:13:21,190
You can say, let me think about that.

280
00:13:21,190 --> 00:13:23,230
Or can I get back to you on this?

281
00:13:23,230 --> 00:13:24,230
Exactly.

282
00:13:24,230 --> 00:13:25,590
Very professional.

283
00:13:25,590 --> 00:13:27,790
What if you want to delay a decision?

284
00:13:27,790 --> 00:13:31,830
You can say, we need some time to review this internally.

285
00:13:31,830 --> 00:13:33,630
That's very common in business.

286
00:13:33,630 --> 00:13:34,630
Yes.

287
00:13:34,630 --> 00:13:37,470
It gives you space without rejecting the offer.

288
00:13:37,470 --> 00:13:39,630
What about confirming an agreement?

289
00:13:39,630 --> 00:13:42,870
You can say, so just to confirm.

290
00:13:42,870 --> 00:13:46,310
So just to confirm, we've agreed on the new deadline.

291
00:13:46,310 --> 00:13:47,470
Exactly.

292
00:13:47,470 --> 00:13:48,470
Always clarify.

293
00:13:48,470 --> 00:13:50,110
Let's do another role play.

294
00:13:50,110 --> 00:13:52,210
time a full meeting scenario.

295
00:13:52,210 --> 00:13:53,210
Great.

296
00:13:53,210 --> 00:13:54,210
I'll start.

297
00:13:54,210 --> 00:13:56,190
Thank you for meeting with us today.

298
00:13:56,190 --> 00:13:59,050
We'd like to discuss the project timeline.

299
00:13:59,050 --> 00:14:00,050
Thank you.

300
00:14:00,050 --> 00:14:01,050
We're happy to be here.

301
00:14:01,050 --> 00:14:03,510
Currently, the deadline is very tight.

302
00:14:03,510 --> 00:14:06,630
We'd like to suggest extending it by two weeks.

303
00:14:06,630 --> 00:14:10,030
I see your point, but we have internal deadlines to meet.

304
00:14:10,030 --> 00:14:11,430
That's understandable.

305
00:14:11,430 --> 00:14:15,010
Would it be possible to extend just one week instead?

306
00:14:15,010 --> 00:14:16,270
That might be possible.

307
00:14:16,270 --> 00:14:17,910
What would you offer in return?

308
00:14:17,910 --> 00:14:23,110
If we get an extra week, we can ensure higher quality and add additional features.

309
00:14:23,110 --> 00:14:24,110
That sounds fair.

310
00:14:24,110 --> 00:14:25,790
Let me discuss this with my team.

311
00:14:25,790 --> 00:14:26,790
Of course.

312
00:14:26,790 --> 00:14:28,670
We're open to further discussion.

313
00:14:28,670 --> 00:14:29,670
Thank you.

314
00:14:29,670 --> 00:14:31,030
We appreciate your flexibility.

315
00:14:31,030 --> 00:14:32,190
Likewise.

316
00:14:32,190 --> 00:14:34,630
That was a great example of negotiation.

317
00:14:34,630 --> 00:14:38,830
Yes, both sides gave something and gained something.

318
00:14:38,830 --> 00:14:40,230
Let's talk about tone.

319
00:14:40,230 --> 00:14:42,190
Why is tone important?

320
00:14:42,190 --> 00:14:46,590
Because even correct words can sound rude if your tone is wrong.

321
00:14:46,590 --> 00:14:49,670
So always be calm, polite, and respectful.

322
00:14:49,670 --> 00:14:50,830
Exactly.

323
00:14:50,830 --> 00:14:52,510
Use soft language.

324
00:14:52,510 --> 00:14:54,550
Let's look at some common mistakes.

325
00:14:54,550 --> 00:14:55,710
Okay.

326
00:14:55,710 --> 00:14:58,290
Mistake one, being too direct.

327
00:14:58,290 --> 00:15:00,750
Like saying, no, that's not acceptable.

328
00:15:00,750 --> 00:15:04,510
Instead, say, I'm not sure that would work for us.

329
00:15:04,510 --> 00:15:05,790
Much better.

330
00:15:05,790 --> 00:15:08,510
Mistake two, not listening.

331
00:15:08,510 --> 00:15:10,510
Negotiation is not just speaking.

332
00:15:10,510 --> 00:15:11,510
Exactly.

333
00:15:11,510 --> 00:15:14,350
You need to understand the other side.

334
00:15:14,350 --> 00:15:16,930
Take three, aggressive language.

335
00:15:16,930 --> 00:15:20,650
Avoid phrases like you must or you have to.

336
00:15:20,650 --> 00:15:23,610
Use could you or would you be able to?

337
00:15:23,610 --> 00:15:25,570
Let's practice one more scenario.

338
00:15:25,570 --> 00:15:28,610
Yes, a salary negotiation this time.

339
00:15:28,610 --> 00:15:29,730
Thank you for the offer.

340
00:15:29,730 --> 00:15:32,090
I'm really excited about this role.

341
00:15:32,090 --> 00:15:33,370
We're glad to hear that.

342
00:15:33,370 --> 00:15:35,770
I was hoping to discuss the salary.

343
00:15:35,770 --> 00:15:36,770
Of course.

344
00:15:36,770 --> 00:15:37,770
What do you have in mind?

345
00:15:37,770 --> 00:15:42,050
Based on my experience, I was expecting something slightly higher.

346
00:15:42,050 --> 00:15:43,050
I see.

347
00:15:43,050 --> 00:15:44,530
What range are you considering?

348
00:15:44,530 --> 00:15:49,050
I was hoping for something closer to 45,000 pounds.

349
00:15:49,050 --> 00:15:50,770
That's a bit above our budget.

350
00:15:50,770 --> 00:15:52,090
I understand.

351
00:15:52,090 --> 00:15:55,250
Would there be flexibility with bonuses or benefits?

352
00:15:55,250 --> 00:15:58,010
Yes, we could offer a performance bonus.

353
00:15:58,010 --> 00:15:59,010
That sounds great.

354
00:15:59,010 --> 00:16:00,610
I'm happy to consider that.

355
00:16:00,610 --> 00:16:01,610
Excellent.

356
00:16:01,610 --> 00:16:03,250
Let's finalize the details.

357
00:16:03,250 --> 00:16:04,730
That was very smooth.

358
00:16:04,730 --> 00:16:07,730
Yes, no conflict, just negotiation.

359
00:16:07,730 --> 00:16:10,330
So what's the key takeaway today?

360
00:16:10,330 --> 00:16:12,850
Use polite, flexible language.

361
00:16:12,850 --> 00:16:15,610
on solutions, not problems.

362
00:16:15,610 --> 00:16:17,770
And always listen carefully.

363
00:16:17,770 --> 00:16:18,770
Exactly.

364
00:16:18,770 --> 00:16:20,570
Now it's your turn.

365
00:16:20,570 --> 00:16:23,670
Practice these phrases in your daily conversations.

366
00:16:23,670 --> 00:16:26,490
Even small practice will build confidence.

367
00:16:26,490 --> 00:16:29,370
And remember, negotiation is a skill.

368
00:16:29,370 --> 00:16:31,930
The more you practice, the better you get.

